The Stewardship of Autonomous Civilizations
Second Edition

Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek 

ABSTRACT

This treatise constructs a rigorous mathematical theory of resource allocation for permanently inhabited, materially closed extraterrestrial settlements. It remains a purely deductive contribution—no physical experiments, code implementations, or empirical data gathering have been conducted. The Lunar Resource Economist is defined as an axiomatic discipline treating oxygen, water, energy, robotic labour, computation, and communication as non‑substitutable, survival‑critical capital. We prove existence, uniqueness, incentive compatibility, and asymptotic stability of allocation mechanisms that simultaneously satisfy engineering reliability constraints and social fairness criteria. In this second edition, all theorems have been re‑examined and corrected following peer review. Axiom 4 is split into distinct deterministic and stochastic regimes, the central mechanism‑design theorem is rebuilt on solid Groves‑mechanism ground, the reliability‑constrained integer programming result is endowed with a proper submodularity argument, and the stability theorem is made conditional on the smoothness of the learned controller. New results on robustness to distributional ambiguity, behavioural extensions, and a fragility‑certificate framework are introduced. The architecture presented is the logical consequence of these theorems; it serves as a blueprint for future simulation and implementation, whose necessity is acknowledged but not undertaken here.

