1. AXIOMATIC FOUNDATION OF THE LUNAR RESOURCE ECONOMY

We define a Lunar Resource Economy as a 6‑tuple

\mathcal{E} = (\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{P}, \mathcal{U}, \mathcal{F})

where

· \mathcal{A} = \{a_1,\dots,a_n\} is a finite set of agents (crew, autonomous robots, scientific payloads).
· \mathcal{R} = \{r_1,\dots,r_m\} is a set of critical resources (O₂, H₂O, electrical energy, robot‑hours, bandwidth, compute cycles, spare parts).
· \mathcal{T} = [0,T] is a finite but possibly uncertain mission horizon.
· \mathcal{P} is a vector of production, recovery, and recycling functions for each resource, with physical laws coupling the flows.
· \mathcal{U} = \{u_i : \mathbb{R}^m_+ \times \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}\} are agent‑specific, concave, non‑satiable utility functions continuous in resource flows.
· \mathcal{F} is the set of admissible allocation functions f : \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}^m_+, subject to physical balance and minimum survival thresholds.

Axiom 1 (Physical Balance). At every instant t, the vector of net resource flows sums to zero after accounting for extraction, consumption, and irreversible leakage: \sum_i \mathbf{c}_i(t) = \mathbf{p}(t) - \mathbf{l}(t), where \mathbf{c}_i(t) is consumption of agent i, \mathbf{p}(t) production, and \mathbf{l}(t) irrecoverable losses.

Axiom 2 (Survival Threshold). For every agent i and resource r, there exists a minimal non‑zero flow \underline{c}_{i,r} > 0 such that u_i becomes unboundedly negative if any flow falls below it. This is enforced as a hard constraint: c_{i,r}(t) \ge \underline{c}_{i,r} for all t.

Axiom 3 (Closure as a Controllable Parameter). The fraction \kappa_r \in [0,1] of consumed resource r that is recovered and returned to the common stock is a deterministic function of instantaneous, irreversible infrastructure investment I_r, with I_r(\kappa_r) strictly convex and I_r(1) = \infty (perfect closure physically unattainable).

Axiom 4 (Disturbance Regimes). Resource supply is subject to two distinct disturbance regimes, selected by the control architecture:

· 4a (Bounded Adversarial Regime): Additive disturbance \mathbf{w}(t) with known compact support \mathcal{W} \subset \mathbb{R}^m, piecewise continuous.
· 4b (Stochastic Diffusion Regime): Additive disturbance follows an Itô process \mathrm{d}\mathbf{w}(t) = \mu(t)\mathrm{d}t + \sigma(t)\mathrm{d}\mathbf{B}(t), where \mathbf{B}(t) is a standard Brownian motion and the coefficients satisfy standard Lipschitz and growth conditions.

The controller knows which regime is active and selects the appropriate stability analysis. No single disturbance description attempts to cover both.

Axiom 5 (Latency). Earth–Moon communication introduces a known, constant round‑trip delay \tau > 0 that forces decentralisation of fast control loops. All local measurements are available with zero delay; remote commands are delayed by \tau.

From these axioms, the central problem is to design an allocation function f \in \mathcal{F} that maximises a social welfare functional while respecting the axioms, and does so in a manner that agents have no incentive to misrepresent their utilities.

