2. CORE THEOREMS AND CORRECTED PROOFS

2.1 Convexity and Certainty Equivalence of the Optimal Closure Ratio

Theorem 2.1 (Closure Convexity and Certainty Equivalence).
For any resource r, if the infrastructure investment I_r(\kappa) is strictly convex and increasing, and the expected cumulative resupply cost C_{\text{resupply}}(\kappa,T) is affine in the leakage fraction 1-\kappa and mission duration T, then the total expected cost J(\kappa,T) = I_r(\kappa) + \mathbb{E}[C_{\text{resupply}}(\kappa,T)] is jointly convex in \kappa and T. Consequently, there exists a unique optimal closure ratio \kappa^*(T) for every deterministic T. Moreover, under this affine cost structure the optimal policy enjoys certainty equivalence: \mathbb{E}[J(\kappa,T)] = J(\kappa, \mathbb{E}[T]), so the optimal \kappa depends on the distribution of T only through its mean.

Proof. Write J(\kappa,T) = I(\kappa) + \alpha T (1-\kappa) with \alpha > 0. Joint convexity follows from the sum of a convex function and an affine function. The unique minimiser \kappa^*(T) = \arg\min I(\kappa) - \alpha T \kappa is a deterministic function. For random T with expectation \bar{T}, \mathbb{E}[J(\kappa,T)] = I(\kappa) + \alpha \bar{T}(1-\kappa) = J(\kappa,\bar{T}), establishing certainty equivalence. ∎

Fragility Remark. Certainty equivalence fails if resupply cost is convex in T (e.g., compounding logistics risk over longer missions). In that case the optimal \kappa depends on the full distribution of T, and a robust optimisation over an ambiguity set of possible mission durations is required. This is treated in Section 2.6.

