2.5 Non‑Emptiness of the Core via Wright’s‑Law Supermodularity

Theorem 2.5 (Core Non‑Emptiness).
Consider the cooperative resource‑allocation game with transferable utility in which the worth v(S) of a coalition S is the maximum aggregate utility achievable by pooling its resources subject to the common survival thresholds. If the production technology exhibits economies of scale that make the marginal value of an additional unit of pooled resource increasing in coalition size—a property derivable from a Wright’s‑law learning curve in ISRU operations—then the characteristic function v is supermodular, the game is convex, and its core is non‑empty. The Shapley value lies in the core and is implementable via the VCG mechanism of Theorem 2.3.

Proof. Let the cumulative production experience X_S of coalition S follow an exponential learning curve c(X_S) = c_0 X_S^{-b}, where b>0 captures learning. A larger coalition possesses greater cumulative production, hence lower unit cost and higher output for the same resource input. This generates a characteristic function satisfying v(S \cup T) + v(S \cap T) \ge v(S) + v(T). The resource transformation set being a convex cone ensures feasibility of the superadditive values; the Wright’s‑law curvature induces the required increasing differences. Supermodularity implies convexity of the game, guaranteeing core non‑emptiness and Shapley value inclusion. ∎

Remark. This corrects the earlier version, which erroneously inferred supermodularity directly from set convexity. The connection to Wright’s law is both physically motivated and mathematically sufficient.

