2.6 Robustness to Distributional Ambiguity (New)

Theorem 2.6 (Fragility‑Certified Allocation).
When the resupply cost is convex in T or the disturbance distribution is uncertain, the optimal closure and spares policies must be robust against an ambiguity set of possible distributions. Define a minimax problem over a Wasserstein ball of radius \rho centered at a nominal distribution. Under moderate conditions, the worst‑case expected cost can be bounded by a convex function, and the resulting robust policy can be computed via a finite‑dimensional convex program. The resulting allocation admits a fragility certificate: a bound on the cost increase if the true distribution lies outside the ball.

Proof. Standard results from distributionally robust optimisation (Mohajerin Esfahani & Kuhn 2018) applied to our cost structure. The dual representation of the Wasserstein ball yields a convex regulariser added to the nominal objective. The fragility certificate is the dual multiplier associated with the radius. ∎

