Appendix A. Convexity of the Optimal Closure Ratio and Certainty Equivalence (Theorem 2.1)

Statement (restated). For any resource r, let infrastructure investment I(\kappa) be strictly convex and increasing in \kappa \in [0,1], with I(1)=\infty. Let the expected cumulative resupply cost be \mathbb{E}[C(\kappa,T)] = \alpha \mathbb{E}[T](1-\kappa) where \alpha>0 is the unit cost of leaked resource per unit time, and T is a non‑negative random variable with finite expectation \bar{T} = \mathbb{E}[T]. Then the total expected cost J(\kappa) = I(\kappa) + \alpha \bar{T}(1-\kappa) is strictly convex in \kappa, attaining a unique minimiser \kappa^*. Moreover, \kappa^* depends on the distribution of T only through \bar{T}; i.e., it exhibits certainty equivalence.

Proof.

1. Convexity:
      The function J(\kappa) = I(\kappa) + \alpha \bar{T} - \alpha \bar{T} \kappa.
      Since I(\kappa) is strictly convex and the affine term -\alpha \bar{T}\kappa does not affect convexity, J is strictly convex.
      Therefore any local minimum is global and unique.
2. First‑order condition:
      The minimiser \kappa^* satisfies I'(\kappa^*) = \alpha \bar{T}. Because I' is increasing (convex I), the solution is unique whenever it exists in (0,1). The boundary I(1)=\infty guarantees an interior solution.
3. Certainty equivalence:
      By linearity of expectation and the affine structure of the resupply cost in T and \kappa,
   \mathbb{E}[J(\kappa,T)] = I(\kappa) + \alpha \mathbb{E}[T](1-\kappa) = J(\kappa, \bar{T}),
   which depends on the distribution of T only through its mean \bar{T}. Hence the optimal \kappa^* is a function of \bar{T} alone. ∎

Fragility Remark (from main text). If C(\kappa,T) is convex in T (e.g., C(\kappa,T) = \gamma T^2 (1-\kappa)), then \mathbb{E}[C(\kappa,T)] \ge \alpha \bar{T}(1-\kappa) by Jensen’s inequality, and certainty equivalence fails. The robust version is treated in Appendix F.

