Appendix D. Stability Under Delay for Smooth Controllers (Theorem 2.4)

We provide the full Lyapunov–Krasovskii stability proof for the deterministic bounded‑disturbance regime (Axiom 4a). The stochastic case is summarised.

D.1 Deterministic Regime

System model. The linearised dynamics are

\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = A\mathbf{x}(t) + B\hat{\mathbf{u}}(t-\tau) + B_w \mathbf{w}(t),

where \mathbf{w}(t) \in \mathcal{W} compact, and \hat{\mathbf{u}} is the output of a Lipschitz‑continuous function approximator:

\hat{\mathbf{u}}(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{u}^*(\mathbf{x}) + \mathbf{e}(\mathbf{x}),\quad \|\mathbf{e}(\mathbf{x})\| \le L\|\mathbf{x}\| + e_0, \quad \forall \mathbf{x} \in \Omega,

with \Omega a forward‑invariant compact set containing the origin. The optimal controller \mathbf{u}^*(\mathbf{x}) stabilises the delay‑free system in the sense that \dot{\mathbf{x}} = A\mathbf{x} + B\mathbf{u}^*(\mathbf{x}) is globally exponentially stable.

Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional. Choose

V(t) = \mathbf{x}^T(t) P \mathbf{x}(t) + \int_{-\tau}^{0} \int_{t+\theta}^{t} \dot{\mathbf{x}}^T(s) Q \dot{\mathbf{x}}(s)\, ds\, d\theta,

with P,Q \succ 0. Differentiating along trajectories yields (using standard Leibniz rule)

\dot{V} = 2\mathbf{x}^T P \dot{\mathbf{x}} + \tau \dot{\mathbf{x}}^T Q \dot{\mathbf{x}} - \int_{t-\tau}^{t} \dot{\mathbf{x}}^T(s) Q \dot{\mathbf{x}}(s)\, ds.

Substitute \dot{\mathbf{x}} = A\mathbf{x} + B\mathbf{u}^*(\mathbf{x}) + B\mathbf{e}(\mathbf{x}) + B_w\mathbf{w} + B[\hat{\mathbf{u}}(t-\tau) - \hat{\mathbf{u}}(t)] etc. We use the descriptor method (Fridman 2014) to obtain an LMI. Introduce the augmented state \xi(t) = \text{col}\{\mathbf{x}(t), \mathbf{x}(t-\tau), \mathbf{e}(\mathbf{x}(t)), \mathbf{w}(t)\}. After bounding the integral term via Jensen’s inequality and using the Lipschitz bound on \mathbf{e}, we obtain a quadratic form:

\dot{V} \le \xi^T \Phi \xi + \epsilon_1 \|\mathbf{w}\|^2,

where \Phi is an LMI condition parameterized by P, Q, \tau. If the LMI \Phi \prec 0 is feasible, then

\dot{V} \le -\lambda_{\min}(-\Phi) \|\xi\|^2 + \epsilon_1 \sup\|\mathbf{w}\|^2.

Because \|\xi\| \ge \|\mathbf{x}\|, this yields \dot{V} \le -\gamma \|\mathbf{x}\|^2 + \delta, with \gamma>0. Hence the system is uniformly ultimately bounded. The maximum allowable delay \tau_{\max} is the supremum of \tau for which the LMI is feasible.

Proof of LMI condition (sketch). The full derivation follows Section 3.2 of Fridman (2014). The term B\hat{\mathbf{u}}(t-\tau) is rewritten as B\mathbf{u}^*(\mathbf{x}(t-\tau)) + B\mathbf{e}(t-\tau). Using the model transformation \dot{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{y} and applying the free‑weighting matrix technique, one arrives at \Phi = \Phi_0 + \tau \Phi_1 < 0. The Lipschitz bound on \mathbf{e} is handled by the S‑procedure: \|\mathbf{e}\|^2 \le L^2\|\mathbf{x}\|^2 + e_0^2 is added as a non‑positive term with a multiplier. The resulting LMI is affine in \tau and thus provides \tau_{\max}.

D.2 Stochastic Regime (Axiom 4b)

When the disturbance is a Brownian motion, the dynamics become an Itô SDE. The stability notion is mean‑square ultimate boundedness. We use a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional of the same form, but compute the infinitesimal generator \mathcal{L}V. The cross‑term with the diffusion yields an additional trace term. The LMI condition is augmented with the noise intensity \sigma. Under standard assumptions, one can show \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}V] \le -\gamma \mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{x}\|^2] + \delta, leading to boundedness in mean square. Details are standard (see Mao 2007, Chapter 6).

Remarks on applicability. The proof relies on the Lipschitz continuity of \hat{\mathbf{u}}. For tree ensembles (non‑smooth), a probabilistic bound via scenario sampling or a smooth surrogate is required; that is not covered by this theorem and is left as future work (see main text).

