Appendix F. Distributionally Robust Closure and Spares Policies (Theorem 2.6)

Problem. The mission horizon T or the disturbance distribution may be uncertain. We consider a robust formulation where the true distribution \mu of T belongs to a Wasserstein ambiguity set \mathcal{M}_\rho = \{\mu : W_p(\mu, \hat{\mu}_0) \le \rho\}, where \hat{\mu}_0 is a nominal distribution and W_p is the p-Wasserstein distance. The goal is to minimise the worst‑case expected cost over \mathcal{M}_\rho.

Theorem. Under mild conditions (cost function is Lipschitz continuous in T), the distributionally robust problem

\min_{\kappa \in [0,1]} \max_{\mu \in \mathcal{M}_\rho} \mathbb{E}_\mu [J(\kappa, T)]

is equivalent to a finite‑dimensional convex program. Moreover, the optimal \kappa^* admits a fragility certificate: the optimal dual variable associated with the Wasserstein radius \rho quantifies the sensitivity of the worst‑case cost to misspecification.

Proof (sketch).
By duality for Wasserstein distributionally robust optimization (Mohajerin Esfahani & Kuhn 2018, Theorem 3), for a fixed \kappa,

\max_{\mu \in \mathcal{M}_\rho} \mathbb{E}_\mu [J(\kappa, T)] = \min_{\lambda \ge 0} \left\{ \lambda \rho^\theta + \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \sup_{\xi} \left( J(\kappa, \xi) - \lambda d(\xi, \hat{\xi}_i)^\theta \right) \right\},

where \hat{\xi}_i are samples from the nominal distribution, \theta is the order of the Wasserstein distance, and d is the metric. Because J(\kappa,T) = I(\kappa) + \alpha T(1-\kappa), the inner supremum for a given \lambda can be solved analytically. The resulting expression is convex in \kappa and piecewise linear. Minimising over \kappa yields a convex program. The dual multiplier \lambda^* provides a certificate: if the true distribution lies within radius \rho, the cost is bounded; the rate of change of the optimal value with respect to \rho is exactly \lambda^*. ∎

