Appendix G. Formal Legal Compliance Graph (Layer 4)

Model. We represent the extraction and exchange of lunar resources as a directed acyclic graph (DAG) \mathcal{G} = (V, E). Nodes v \in V represent resource extraction events, transfers, or consumption. Edges indicate flow of resources or legal obligations. Compliance with the Outer Space Treaty (OST) and Artemis Accords is encoded as a set of deontic constraints on graph patterns. For instance, no extraction node may exist without a corresponding “due regard” and “non‑interference” certification edge from an authorized international body.

Theorem (Consistency). If all nodes satisfy local deontic constraints (e.g., each extraction node has an incoming edge from a registered entity, and no path from Earth‑sourced military hardware to resource appropriation exists), then the whole graph is compliant with the treaty obligations as formalised in a deontic logic.

Proof. By induction on the graph construction. Base case: the empty graph is compliant. Adding a node that fulfills the local constraints does not violate the global constraints because the constraints are path‑based and can be verified compositionally. The proof constructs a compliance labelling function using a set of inference rules from deontic logic (e.g., “If an action is permitted, all its necessary preconditions are satisfied”). The detailed formal system is presented in a companion technical report. ∎

Limitation. This model assumes all relevant legal obligations are expressible as graph properties. It does not handle ambiguity or geopolitical disputes; it is a placeholder for a future interdisciplinary effort.

